    "words": "372 INLAID PRECIOUS STONES",
    "caption": "Buried in total darkness for four thousand years, an incredible royal treasure finally re-emerged to stun the modern world.\n\nThe pectoral of Senusret II features an astonishing 372 cloisons, each meticulously inlaid with turquoise, lapis lazuli, and carnelian. It was uncovered alongside exquisite cosmetic vessels and a royal crown that had not seen the light for four thousand years. Detail photos even reveal a close up of a bird resting on a stand.\n\nIn hieroglyphs, Egyptian listings for pectoral jewelry utilized the word usekh, which translates to 'breadth' or 'to be wide'. A similar pectoral of Amenemhat III distinctly states his prenomen Ny-Maat-Ra along with his hieroglyphic titles. E. A. Wallis Budge's dictionary tracks words for these pectorals across roughly 200 ancient works and 120 authors.\n\nToday, these artifacts are so vital to scholarship that Janice Kamrin's modern hieroglyph primer features the pectoral of Amenemhat III in Exercise 22, Object 3. They serve as essential tools for linguists and historians studying ancient royal culture.\n\n📖 Read the full story: https://www.zalavip.com/blog/the-extraordinary-four-thousand-year-old-royal-egyptian-pectoral-2026-11-13\n\n🔔 Follow us for a true story from Ancient Egypt every day.\n\n🌐 https://www.zalavip.com\n\n#AncientEgypt #Egypt #History #historyartifacts #royaljewelry",
